answer:
To determine the ordered pair $(a, b)$ such that the matrices

$$
A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -8 \\ a & 11 \end{pmatrix}, \quad B = \begin{pmatrix} 11 & b \\ 4 & 3 \end{pmatrix}
$$

are inverses of each other, we use the definition of matrix inverses: if $A$ and $B$ are inverses, then both $AB = I$ and $BA = I$, where $I$ is the identity matrix:

$$
I = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
$$

---

### Step 1: Compute $AB$

We compute the product $AB$:

$$
AB = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -8 \\ a & 11 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 11 & b \\ 4 & 3 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
3 \cdot 11 + (-8) \cdot 4 & 3 \cdot b + (-8) \cdot 3 \\
a \cdot 11 + 11 \cdot 4 & a \cdot b + 11 \cdot 3
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
33 - 32 & 3b - 24 \\
11a + 44 & ab + 33
\end{pmatrix}
$$

Set this equal to the identity matrix:

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
33 - 32 & 3b - 24 \\
11a + 44 & ab + 33
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
$$

This gives us the system of equations:

1. $33 - 32 = 1$ (already true)
2. $3b - 24 = 0 \Rightarrow b = 8$
3. $11a + 44 = 0 \Rightarrow a = -4$
4. $ab + 33 = 1$

Check the last equation with $a = -4$ and $b = 8$:

$$
ab + 33 = (-4)(8) + 33 = -32 + 33 = 1 \quad \text{✓}
$$

---

### Step 2: Compute $BA$

Now compute the product $BA$:

$$
BA = \begin{pmatrix} 11 & b \\ 4 & 3 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -8 \\ a & 11 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
11 \cdot 3 + b \cdot a & 11 \cdot (-8) + b \cdot 11 \\
4 \cdot 3 + 3 \cdot a & 4 \cdot (-8) + 3 \cdot 11
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
33 + ab & -88 + 11b \\
12 + 3a & -32 + 33
\end{pmatrix}
$$

Substitute $a = -4$ and $b = 8$:

$$
BA = \begin{pmatrix}
33 + (-32) & -88 + 88 \\
12 - 12 & -32 + 33
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
$$

This confirms that both $AB = I$ and $BA = I$, so the matrices are indeed inverses.

---

### Final Answer

$$
\boxed{(-4, 8)}
$$

